      "source": "en_US",
      "content": "Use natural American vocabulary and syntax within the selected genre's voice and structure. American English permits direct, active phrasing without requiring short sentences, an early conclusion or a promotional tone.\n\nCollective nouns take singular agreement: *the team is meeting*, *the committee has decided*, *the band is on tour*. The plural agreement of British usage reads as a foreign import here.\n\nThe simple past covers a recent past: *I just saw the figures*, *did you eat?* The present perfect is available but is not the default in those positions.\n\n*Gotten* is the standard past participle in the sense of acquiring or becoming: *it's gotten worse*, *we've gotten three replies*. *Got* is retained for possession: *I've got it*.\n\nPrepositions: *on the weekend*, *in the hospital*, *different from* or *different than*, *write somebody*, *meet with somebody* in the sense of holding a meeting.\n\nThe serial comma is standard in most American house styles and omitted in news style. Pick per document and hold it.\n\nThe mandative subjunctive is alive in formal American prose and is not an archaism: *the board recommends that he be reinstated*, *it is essential that she attend*."
    },
    "review": {
      "source": "en_US",
      "content": "Read for British spellings and vocabulary that have drifted in: *colour*, *centre*, *organise*, *whilst*, *amongst*, *autumn* for fall, *lift* for elevator, *flat* for apartment.\n\nRead for house-style drift within one document. Chicago and AP disagree on the serial comma, on title capitalization, on when numbers are spelled out, and on how times and states are abbreviated. A document that follows both has followed neither, and this is the most common structural defect in assembled American copy.\n\nAmerican business prose collapses into abstract nouns faster than most: *leverage*, *utilize*, *operationalize*, *impactful*, *actionable*, *learnings*, *bandwidth*, *alignment*. Ask in each case what somebody actually did, and write that.\n\nCheck headings for one capitalization system. Title case and sentence case both occur in American publishing; alternating between them within a document does not.\n\nWatch for intensifier inflation — *incredibly*, *absolutely*, *literally*, *truly*, *hugely* — which American register tolerates more than British and which accumulates until nothing in the text is emphatic because everything is.\n\nCheck number style: whether numbers under ten are spelled out, whether percentages are written as digits, and whether the same rule holds across the whole document."
    },
    "anti-slop": {
      "source": "en_US",
      "content": "The reskinning tell in the other direction. Generated American English is often British English with the spelling changed, and the leftovers are formats and idiom rather than spelling: *color* and *organize* in a text that still writes *at the weekend*, *£*, *24 August*, and *Mr* without a period.\n\nThe corporate-American register a model defaults to when asked for American English: *leverage*, *unlock*, *supercharge*, *seamless*, *robust*, *best-in-class*, *move the needle*, *circle back*, *at the end of the day*, *double down*, *take it to the next level*. These arrive as a set rather than because any one of them was meant.\n\n*Utilize* for *use*, *incentivize* for *encourage*, *operationalize* for *do*. The longer verb is not the more precise one, and its density is a tell rather than a register.\n\nEm dash density. The unspaced em dash is correct American punctuation; what marks generated prose is one in every paragraph, used to attach an afterthought the sentence did not need — like this, repeatedly.\n\nHouse-style inconsistency within a document — the serial comma present in one list and absent in the next, *p.m.* in one time and *pm* in another — is a generation artefact rather than a choice, and worth naming as such rather than silently normalizing, because it usually means the passage came from somewhere else.\n\nThe listicle reflex in running prose: every paragraph opening with an ordinal or a bolded label, and every idea presented as a numbered takeaway whether or not the argument has parts."
